Illegal websites every web designer should know.

Most designers and developers I know get stuck in a loop of trying to reinvent every button, hover effect, or shader they see online. It usually leads to a messy codebase and a site that takes way too long to ship. I found three specific resources that help me skip the manual labor while keeping the final product looking professional.

designprompts.dev is my go-to for sparking ideas. It offers a huge library of styles, from monochrome to clean SaaS layouts, and allows you to copy design prompts with a single click. It saves you from that staring-at-a-blank-screen feeling.

Next is Animmaster Library. It hosts over 200 animated components and a massive collection of portfolio examples. If you need a specific interaction, it is probably already built and ready to drop into your code.

Finally, I have been playing with Aceternity UI. It provides over 300 premium components that are actually easy to integrate. I recently tested their cloud shader, and the result was surprisingly sharp for how little effort it took to set up. You just copy the JavaScript and it works.

I stopped trying to be a hero with every single line of CSS. Now I use these to build faster and spend my energy on the actual product logic.

Which resources do you keep in your bookmarks for when you are stuck on a UI problem?

#webdesign #frontend #developer #productivity
